  tocItems={tocItems}
  relatedArticles={relatedArticles}
>
  <p>The default media players built into Windows (Movies & TV) and macOS (QuickTime) are heavily restricted. They often fail to play modern MKV files or videos encoded with H.265. To get a seamless playback experience, you should use a dedicated, open-source media player.</p>

  <h2 id="vlc">VLC Media Player (Best Overall)</h2>
  <p>VLC is the undisputed king of media players. Developed by the VideoLAN non-profit organization, it is free, open-source, and has virtually every codec known to man built right into the software.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Plays absolutely anything. Available on Windows, Mac, Linux, iOS, and Android. Built-in subtitle downloader.</li>
    <li><strong>Cons:</strong> The UI feels a bit dated on modern operating systems.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="mpv">MPV (Best for Enthusiasts)</h2>
  <p>MPV is a free, open-source, cross-platform media player designed for the minimalist. It has almost no graphical user interface; instead, it overlays a small, sleek control bar directly onto the video. It uses incredibly advanced scaling algorithms to make videos look sharper than they do in VLC.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Superior video scaling quality, minimalist UI, highly customizable via text files.</li>
    <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Steep learning curve; most settings require editing a config file rather than clicking a menu.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="mpc-hc">MPC-HC (Best for Old Windows PCs)</h2>
  <p>Media Player Classic - Home Cinema (MPC-HC) is a lightweight, open-source media player for Windows. It mimics the look of the classic Windows Media Player 6.4 but is powered by modern decoding engines.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Incredibly lightweight, making it perfect for old laptops or low-end PCs that struggle to play HD video in VLC.</li>
    <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Windows only. Official development stopped in 2017 (though community forks still exist).</li>
  </ul>
</ArticleLayout>
